{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 and one of the most influential nobles in the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. He was born in 1140 the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His mother was the daughter of King Baldwin II which gave him a strong claim to the throne of Jerusalem. Raymond became count at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated in 1152. Because of his youth a regency was established under his mother and later under his uncle Joscelin II. During this time Tripoli was often under threat from Muslim forces and internal instability. Raymond eventually took full control of his county in 1157 and began to assert his authority.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III was known for his political acumen and his deep involvement in the affairs of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He served as regent of the kingdom twice first during the minority of Baldwin IV and later during the illness of Baldwin V. His regencies were marked by attempts to stabilize the kingdom which was suffering from internal divisions and external threats. Raymond was often at odds with other nobles especially the military orders and the king’s mother Agnes of Courtenay. He was a strong advocate for the rights of the nobility and resisted centralized royal power. His rivalry with the king’s court led to periods of exile and political marginalization but he always returned to influence events.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a critical role in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the few nobles who opposed the reckless campaign led by Guy of Lusignan. Raymond warned against attacking Saladin’s forces without adequate preparation and tried to negotiate a truce. His caution was ignored and he was forced to join the crusader army. At the battle he commanded the left wing but the crusaders were decisively defeated. Raymond escaped the battlefield and returned to Tripoli where he died in 1187 shortly after the fall of Jerusalem. His death marked the end of an era of noble leadership in the crusader states. Raymond III is remembered as a capable administrator and a cautious statesman who understood the fragility of the Latin East. His legacy is one of diplomacy and resistance to rash military action.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.9740936160087585}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -3.194758028257638e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -7.271740287251305e-06}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.2927336096763611}, {"token": " one", "logprob": -1.08825